BUG-38: documents with thousands of anchored objects (our own PDF -> DOCX output) --
three superlinear costs made linear, measured with a named CPU profile and counters.

1. SdrObjList order numbers stay valid (svx/source/svdraw/svdpage.cxx, svdpage.hxx).
   Every insert and every move set mbObjOrdNumsDirty, so the next GetOrdNum() renumbered the
   whole page (RecalcObjOrdNums) and the text-box z-order correction
   (SdrObject::ensureSortedImmediatelyAfter) took its scan-the-page branch: per DOCX here,
   29-204 M renumber steps and 99-898 M scan steps. Now an append to a valid list leaves it
   valid (NbcInsertObject already meant to: the container call defeated it), and
   SetObjectOrdNum / SetExistingObjectOrdNum renumber only the moved range on a valid list.
   With upstream 22bffed4daf5 (tdf#125846, removing the last object does not renumber),
   backported. The order numbers are the same numbers RecalcObjOrdNums would assign.
2. GraphicZOrderHelper::findZOrder (writerfilter) searched its ordered std::map with a linear
   std::find_if; now lower_bound / upper_bound (the same predicates). pdf-docx writes ascending
   relativeHeight, so every shape walked the whole map: 12.3 s of a 16,762-shape import.
3. SwSortedObjs::Insert ran is_sorted() (O(n), a heavy comparator) on every insert, so frame
   creation for n objects anchored in one paragraph or on one page was O(n^2): 52 s, 53% of
   view creation, for 5,878 anchors in one paragraph (the time the no-progress watchdog
   stopped). SwSortedObjs::BulkInsertScope around AppendObjs: within it a list is checked once,
   on its first insert; frame creation changes no sort criterion between inserts, and a sorted
   list stays sorted under lower-bound insertion. When the outermost scope ends, every list it
   touched is checked again and re-sorted if needed, as Insert() would have.

Not WASM-specific (no #ifdef): correct on desktop too, and candidates for upstream (tdf#169116).
Documents module only for now (where it is measured).

